messages:
Maya (presales): Quick thread: AuroraBank wants a Rust sample using WebSocket streaming + function calls + strict JSON schema validation. They also flagged procurement questions (SOC2, invoice cadence) and CMEK/KMS keys. Can eng confirm feasible POC flow?
Omar (eng): Rust SDK is ready but the WS handshake for tool-calls needs a small modification to support server-initiated callbacks (we use a subprotocol header currently). We can ship a minimal example that opens a stream and registers callback handlers.
Maya (presales): They asked specifically about heartbeat/keepalive behavior for long-lived sessions (overnight batch streams). Any recommended settings?
Omar (eng): Recommend 30s ping/pong plus an app-level heartbeat JSON message every 5m. If idle >10m we mark session stale and allow resumable KV cache if client provides resume token. Sample: ```
// client heartbeat
{"type":"heartbeat","ts":1670000000}
```
Priya (solutions): For structured output, suggest: (1) strict JSON schema enforced server-side, (2) on schema failure the runtime returns a structured error with snippet, (3) auto-retry with schema hinting, (4) final fallback to text with annotation. We'll include a prompt template that forces deterministic serialization.
Tobias (security): CMEK is available for Dedicated and Private; for Hosted we require a procurement review for CMEK enablement. Also need SAML metadata and the vendor security questionnaire completed. Is AuroraBank doing an on-prem KMS connector?
Maya (presales): They want customer-managed keys if possible, but first POC can use hosted with audit logs. Procurement asked about invoice terms (net-30 vs net-45) and a temporary POC discount for the bank's security review period.
Jules (sales-ops): We can offer a 30-day POC credit on Dedicated trials and Net-45 for enterprise procurement in some regions. I'll draft a PO template and include the SOC2 report link.
Priya (solutions): Telemetry: include a trace_id in every client message header and log-level sampling (1% full traces, 100% error traces). We'll provide a tracing snippet for Rust that sets trace_id and session_id.
Omar (eng): Minimal Rust example: ```
let mut ws = connect("wss://api.redwood.ai/v1/stream", Some("redwood.v1.toolcall"));
ws.send(serde_json::to_string(&handshake).unwrap());
// listen for server toolcall: {"type":"tool_call","name":"lookup","id":"abc123"}
// respond with: {"type":"tool_result","id":"abc123","result":{...}}
```
Maya (presales): Good. Also they want an example of ephemeral tokens rotated via a refresh endpoint; can we provide a tiny lambda snippet they can host?
Omar (eng): Yes — we have a 20-line lambda example in Node/Python. For Rust customers we recommend a small token proxy; we'll add a Docker-ready example.
Tobias (security): Action: AuroraBank to provide SAML IdP metadata and an example admin user. Also need the KMS access matrix - I've sent the questionnaire doc link.
Jules (sales-ops): Pricing callouts: expected 100k requests/day with 40 concurrent streaming sessions -> approx $9k/mo hosted; Dedicated reserved node estimate $12k/mo with SLA. I'll include EU-eu-west region uplift and egress/ bandwidth assumptions.
Maya (presales): Please include a note on burst capacity and how fallback to smaller model family affects cost.
Priya (solutions): POC success criteria draft: (A) end-to-end toolcall roundtrip <350ms 95th, (B) schema pass rate >=99% on validation tests, (C) session resume works after transient disconnects, (D) telemetry hooks show per-session cost breakdown.
deploy-bot: streaming-handler v2.1 deployed to eu-west, smoke tests ok. :rocket:
Maya (presales): Timeline: Omar to post Rust sample + heartbeat sample by Tue EOD, Priya to post runbook and schema templates, Jules to send pricing/PO template Thu AM, Tobias to confirm security checklist items with Aurora by EOD Tue. I'll follow up with Aurora for SAML metadata. :thumbsup:
